WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.200
﻿Feynman diagrams are very useful&nbsp;
because they take the complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.000
and abstract mathematics of particle interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.960
and show the equations as simple pictures of&nbsp;
what happens when subatomic particles interact.
&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.720
The axes of feynman diagrams are space and time,&nbsp;
and the different particles get different lines:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.120
a photon is a wiggle, the gluons are&nbsp;
spirals, the W and Z bosons and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.200
Higgs boson get a dotted line, and the&nbsp;
fermions get straight lines with arrows.
&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.000
These arrows point forwards in time for matter&nbsp;
particles and backwards in time for antimatter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.240
particles, and the arrows always flow from&nbsp;
one side of the diagram to the other.
&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.240
The building blocks of Feynman diagrams are&nbsp;
vertices which are a joining point between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.280
three particles. The orientation of these&nbsp;
vertices changes the story they are telling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.880
This one shows an electron changing&nbsp;
momentum when it emits a photon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.360
But if we rotate it, it shows an electron&nbsp;
and a positron annihilation into a photon.
&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.360
You can build most Feynman diagrams&nbsp;
from this set of vertices, like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.280
or this. If you want to be able to make sense of&nbsp;
these check out my full video in the description.

